{"surfaces":[{"id":"matisse","label":"MATISSE reconstruction","falseColor":false,"coverage":"One image of the photosphere reconstructed with the public SQUEEZE code from 780 squared visibilities and 520 closure phases measured by VLT/MATISSE on 8 and 19 February 2020 in the 3.94-4.00 micrometre pseudo-continuum, convolved to the 4 milliarcsecond interferometric beam and cast onto a sphere of the published radius. The camera is computed: the rotation pole lies in the plane of the sky at the ALMA position angle, the display meridian is the sub-Earth point at the epoch, and the pixel scale is the reconstruction's. Nothing finer than the beam is shown: the raw reconstruction carries sub-beam texture the visibilities never constrained. Negating every closure phase fits the data equally well, so which side of the star is brighter is weakly constrained. Grayscale is reconstructed relative intensity in one infrared band, not colour or temperature. The far hemisphere is unobserved.","observation":{"schema":"cssearth-surface-observation-report@1","format":"controlled-shape-camera","camera":{"kind":"control-network","positionKm":[5183938336905497,0,0]},"frames":[{"id":"matisse-2020-02-continuum","startTime":"2020-02-08T00:12:31","filter":"L-band pseudo-continuum 3.942-3.974 and 3.992-3.998 um","camera":{"kind":"control-network","observerLatitude":0,"observerWestLongitude":0,"sunLatitude":0,"sunWestLongitude":0,"rangeKm":5183938336905497,"northAzimuthDegrees":312,"pixelAngleMicroradians":0.003781546712654381,"center":[63.994,63.001]},"geometry":{"source":"source-mesh-rays","projectedBounds":[34,33,94,93],"geometryPixels":2313,"unconstrainedPixels":0,"normals":"interpolated welded vertex normals"},"quality":{"encoding":"fits-oi-reconstruction","rasterOffset":2880,"background":{"offset":0,"maximum":0.00001},"coverageInsetPixels":0,"withheldPixels":{"quality":0,"background":10117,"coverageInset":0},"silhouette":{"litPixels":2032,"onSkyPixels":0,"share":0,"maximumShare":0.25}},"pix